1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,467
Installing a Flatpak app pulls in more than the app: a shared runtime comes with it.

2
00:00:05,487 --> 00:00:07,889
Watch what actually lands on the system.

3
00:00:08,359 --> 00:00:12,222
In this video: install and remove Flatpak software packages.

4
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:19,325
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:22,612
Install and remove Flatpak software packages.

6
00:00:23,082 --> 00:00:26,950
We'll search for an app, install it, see what was installed, remove it,

7
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:29,633
clean up what is left over, and install it again.

8
00:00:37,153 --> 00:00:40,355
A Flatpak app does not carry everything it needs.

9
00:00:40,375 --> 00:00:43,315
It uses a runtime, a shared set of libraries,

10
00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:45,880
installed once and reused by many apps.

11
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:56,329
So installing one app installs the runtime too.

12
00:00:56,349 --> 00:01:00,732
And removing the app leaves the runtime behind, until we clean it up.

13
00:01:07,252 --> 00:01:11,305
We start with the remote from the last video, flathub, already added.

14
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:13,667
flatpak search looks for an app by name.

15
00:01:13,687 --> 00:01:18,389
Right now it finds nothing, because the search data has not been downloaded yet.

16
00:01:23,859 --> 00:01:29,044
flatpak update with --appstream downloads that search data, and it needs sudo.

17
00:01:29,064 --> 00:01:31,647
Searching again lists many calculators.

18
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:36,519
The columns are the name, a description, the application id, the version,

19
00:01:36,539 --> 00:01:39,989
the branch, and the remote. The output is shortened.

20
00:01:48,509 --> 00:01:54,074
We pick the GNOME Calculator. Its application id is org.gnome.Calculator.

21
00:01:54,094 --> 00:01:57,975
flatpak install takes the remote name, then the id.

22
00:01:57,995 --> 00:02:02,718
-y answers yes to the question, and sudo makes it system-wide.

23
00:02:07,188 --> 00:02:12,705
Look at the plan. Seven items, not one. The calculator itself is only two megabytes.

24
00:02:12,725 --> 00:02:17,714
The big one is the GNOME platform, the runtime, over four hundred megabytes.

25
00:02:17,734 --> 00:02:20,416
The progress bars are cut from the video.

26
00:02:26,936 --> 00:02:31,655
flatpak list shows everything installed: the app, and the runtimes it needs.

27
00:02:31,675 --> 00:02:33,401
The last column says system.

28
00:02:36,871 --> 00:02:40,819
Add --app to see only applications. Just the calculator.

29
00:02:43,289 --> 00:02:46,805
flatpak info with the application id shows the details:

30
00:02:46,825 --> 00:02:51,048
the version, the license, where it came from, the runtime it uses,

31
00:02:51,068 --> 00:02:52,912
and the installation, system.

32
00:02:59,432 --> 00:03:05,439
flatpak uninstall removes an app. Same pattern: sudo, -y, and the application id.

33
00:03:05,459 --> 00:03:08,564
It removes the app and its language files.

34
00:03:12,034 --> 00:03:17,474
flatpak list --app is now empty. But flatpak list still shows the runtimes.

35
00:03:17,494 --> 00:03:20,313
The app is gone, its libraries are not.

36
00:03:25,833 --> 00:03:31,859
Add --unused to uninstall, and Flatpak removes every runtime that no app needs

37
00:03:31,879 --> 00:03:35,114
anymore. Five items go. The list is empty.

38
00:03:42,634 --> 00:03:46,246
Now we install the calculator again, the same command.

39
00:03:46,266 --> 00:03:50,415
The runtime has to be downloaded again, because we removed it.

40
00:03:50,435 --> 00:03:54,518
The output is shortened. flatpak list --app shows it is back.

41
00:04:04,038 --> 00:04:07,474
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

42
00:04:08,994 --> 00:04:12,749
Installed Flatpak apps live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

43
00:04:12,769 --> 00:04:15,246
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

44
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:23,027
After a fresh boot, flatpak list --app still shows the calculator.

45
00:04:30,547 --> 00:04:36,913
Your turn. Add the Flathub remote system-wide, install org.gnome.Calculator system-wide,

46
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:39,380
and make sure it is the only copy.

47
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:45,668
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

48
00:04:51,188 --> 00:04:55,968
Next up: the shell scripting objectives, starting with creating simple shell scripts.

49
00:04:56,438 --> 00:04:59,341
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
